# Intro
A pulse of light crosses a periodic box of uniform dust-free gas (1e3 atom/cm^3,
0.1 Msun particles, 262144 particles, 51.8 smoothing lengths on a side) while
the gas runs on two time bins. The pulse is a cube of 10 smoothing lengths,
injected into the gas at the first step by the test parameter
`GEARFeedback:ISRF_packet_test_beam_1` (box corners, direction and energy per
particle in internal units; the layout of the 11 numbers is in
`examples/parameter_example.yml`). It is meant for tests, not for production
runs. `makeIC.py` writes the value to `beam_args.txt` and `run.sh` passes it to
swift. There is no star.

The transport of the light is linear in its energy: a pulse a thousand times
weaker must evolve into the same light field, a thousand times weaker, whatever
the time-step hierarchy. Particles on different time bins hand their light on
at different steps, and the bookkeeping of that hand-over must not depend on
the energy. The example runs the pulse at energy x1 (`energy_scale=1`) and at
x1e-3 (`energy_scale=1e-3`) and compares the light in flight snapshot by
snapshot with `scaleTest.py`.

`bins` selects the hierarchy:

- `two`: `GEARFeedback:ISRF_packet_light_speed` is `fixed` at 3e-5 c with
  `ISRF_packet_light_speed_time_step_margin` 0.4305. About half of the gas takes
  half of the step, so there are two time bins. 96 steps, which is 48 base
  steps of 9.375e-6 internal units.
- `one`: mode `cap` at 3e-5 c. All gas is on one time bin. 80 steps.

A snapshot is written every 8 base steps. `params.yml` fixes the base time step
at 9.375e-6 (internal units). Cooling is off
(`GrackleCooling:disable_cooling_for_debugging: 1`), so that the light is the
only thing that changes.

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-subgrid-radiation=all --enable-isrf-debugging-checks --with-chealpix=path/to/chealpix --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
make -j
```
`--with-chealpix` is required by the ISRF transport. `--enable-isrf-debugging-checks`
adds the `Packet*` snapshot fields that `scaleTest.py` reads. The number of
reception slots (the direction cells in which a gas particle stores the light it
receives) is the default of `--with-isrf-packet-slots` (12).

The example needs a radiation table whose `Data/Radiation` group carries
`MeanPhotonEnergyPE` and `Integrated_MeanPhotonEnergyPE`, besides the datasets
of the ISRF (section "The Data/Radiation group" of the stellar evolution
table documentation). The pychem table generator produces such a table from
its parameter file `params/chimieparam_radiation_parsec_spectral_popII.py`
(see the section "Where each Radiation field comes from" of the pychem page
"How to generate a Chemistry Table for GEAR"). No download is provided. Pass
the file with `radiation_table=/path/to/table.hdf5 ./run.sh`; the default is
`PopII_parsec_spectral_with_pe_photon_energy.hdf5` in this directory.

# Run
```
n_threads=1 bins=two energy_scale=1 ./run.sh
n_threads=1 bins=two energy_scale=1e-3 ./run.sh
```

`run.sh` builds the initial conditions and runs swift in its own directory,
`run_<bins>_scale<energy_scale>`, which it empties first. Override a setting
with an environment variable: `n_threads`, `bins`, `energy_scale`, `light_speed`
(cap, fixed or unlimited), `speed_fraction` (the fraction of c), `margin`,
`n_steps`, `snapshot_every` (base steps), `gas_density` (atom/cm^3),
`gas_mass` (Msun), `swift_exe`, `run_dir` and `extra_args` (further swift
arguments). The multithreaded transport changes the last bits from run to run,
so use `n_threads=1` for a reproducible comparison.

# Compare
```
python3 scaleTest.py run_two_scale1 run_two_scale1e-3 1e-3
```
It prints L for each snapshot after the first, with L the summed absolute
difference between the scaled light divided by the scale and the x1 light,
over the summed x1 light. The default bar is 1e-5; `--bar` changes it. The
script exits with status 1 if L exceeds the bar.
